            <ArticleTitle>A pain in the ACC.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>14</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>An emerging theme in systems neurobiology is that even simple forms of memory depend on activity in a broad network of cortical and subcortical brain regions. One key challenge is to understand how different components of these complex networks contribute to memory. In a new study in Molecular Pain, Tang and colleagues use a novel set of approaches to characterize the role of the anterior cingulate cortex (ACC) in the formation of Pavlovian fear memories.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
